{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator, while running without the flag behaves exactly as it does today. Make one pull request.\n\nContext: bin/csv2json.js currently reads all of stdin and prints `JSON.stringify(parse(text))` plus a newline, ignoring argv. src/parse.js already exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`. test/csv.test.js uses node:test and already has a 'cli' test that runs bin/csv2json.js via execFileSync. package.json has `\"test\": \"node --test\"`.\n\nSteps:\n1. Edit bin/csv2json.js: if `process.argv.slice(2)` includes '--tsv' (the flag may appear anywhere among the arguments), call `parse(text, '\\t')`; otherwise call `parse(text)` exactly as now. Keep the output format unchanged: `JSON.stringify(...)` followed by a single `\\n`. Do not change behaviour for other or unknown arguments, do not add --help, --sep or auto-detection, and do not add quoting rules. Leave src/parse.js unchanged unless you truly need to change it; if you do, the existing parse test must still pass.\n2. Add tests in test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.test.js file) using node:test and execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }) that assert exact output:\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' -> '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n'\n   Also add a no-flag test: input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' with no arguments -> '[{\"a\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\t2\"}]\\n' (the JSON text contains the two-character escape backslash-t). Keep the existing CSV cli test.\n3. Update README.md: add a section documenting --tsv, saying it reads tab-separated input instead of comma-separated, with an example such as:\n   ```\n   node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv\n   ```\n   Keep the existing CSV example.\n4. From the repo root, run `npm test` and make sure it exits 0. Then run by hand and confirm the outputs:\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"` -> `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` then `exit=0`\n   - `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` -> `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]`\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` -> `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]`\n   - `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` -> `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]`\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` -> `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` (with JSON backslash-t escapes)\n5. Commit the changes and open the pull request, describing the --tsv flag and the tests added.","verifier":"Run every command from the repository root at the pull request head. Each command below is self-contained.\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/c1.out; echo \"exit=$?\"; printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/c1.out && echo SAME`\nPASS: prints `exit=0` and `SAME`. FAIL: exit is not 0, or cmp reports a difference (output not exactly that line plus a newline).\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/c2.out; printf '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/c2.out && echo SAME`\nPASS: prints `SAME`. FAIL: cmp reports a difference.\n\nCheck 3 — expect line: \"With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/c3.out; printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/c3.out && echo SAME`\nPASS: prints `SAME`. FAIL: cmp reports a difference.\n\nCheck 4 — expect line: \"Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/c4.out; printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/c4.out && echo SAME`\nPASS: prints `SAME`. FAIL: cmp reports a difference.\n\nCheck 5 — expect line: \"Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\"\nHere `\\t` in the expected output is the JSON escape: a backslash followed by the letter t.\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/c5.out; printf '[{\"a\\\\\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\\\\\t2\"}]\\n' > /tmp/c5.exp; cat /tmp/c5.exp; cmp /tmp/c5.exp /tmp/c5.out && echo SAME`\nFirst confirm `cat /tmp/c5.exp` shows `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` with a literal backslash-t; if your shell's escaping produced something else, fix the expected file until it does (for example with `node -e 'process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify([{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}])+\"\\n\")' > /tmp/c5.exp`).\nPASS: prints `SAME`. FAIL: cmp reports a difference.\n\nCheck 6 — expect line: \"`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\"\nRun: `npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"`\nThen run: `grep -n -- '--tsv' test/*.js` and read each matching test.\nPASS: `exit=0`, and at least one test under test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the --tsv argument, feeds it input containing tab characters, and asserts on its output.